ent](#infobox)\n\n##### Report GitHub Issue\n\n×\n\nTitle: \n\nContent selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n\nDescription:\n\nSubmit without GitHub Submit in GitHub\n\n[](https://arxiv.org/IgnoreMe)[![archive](https://static.arxiv.org/static/design-system/latest/logos/arxiv-logo.svg)](https://arxiv.org/)\n\n[Why HTML?](https://info.arxiv.org/about/accessible_HTML.html) Report Issue [Back to Abstract](/abs/2601.16675v1 \"Back to abstract page\") [Download PDF](/pdf/2601.16675v1 \"Download PDF\") Contents Reading mode Colour theme\n\n1.  [Abstract](#abstract1 \"In I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n2.  [1 Introduction](#S1 \"In I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n3.  [2 Background](#S2 \"In I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n    1.  [Fourier Transform ℱ\\\\mathcal{F}.](#S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1 \"In 2 Background ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n4.  [3 Causality in Audio](#S3 \"In I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n5.  [4 freqrex](#S4 \"In I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n6.  [5 Manipulating and Attacking Sufficiencies](#S5 \"In I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n7.  [6 Evaluation](#S6 \"In I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n    1.  [6.1 Subset Manipulations](#S6.SS1 \"In 6 Evaluation ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n8.  [7 Related Work](#S7 \"In I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n9.  [8 Conclusions and Future Work](#S8 \"In I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n10.  [References](#bib \"In I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")\n\n[License: CC BY 4.0](https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available)\n\narXiv:2601.16675v1 \\[cs.SD\\] 23 Jan 2026\n\nI Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:  \nCausal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\n==================================================================================\n\nDavid A. Kelly Note: Contact Author Affiliation: King’s College London Email: [{david.a.kelly](mailto:%7Bdavid.a.kelly)    Hana Chockler Affiliation: King’s College London Email: [hana.chockler}@kcl.ac.uk](mailto:<redacted>)\n\n###### Abstract\n\nIt is well-known that audio classifiers often rely on non-musically relevant features and spurious correlations to classify audio. Hence audio classifiers are easy to manipulate or confuse, resulting in wrong classifications. While inducing a misclassification is not hard, until now the set of features that the classifiers rely on was not well understood. In this paper we introduce a new method that uses causal reasoning to discover features of the frequency space that are sufficient and necessary for a given classification. We describe an implementation of this algorithm in the tool freqrex and provide experimental results on a number of standard benchmark datasets. Our experiments show that causally sufficient and necessary subsets allow us to manipulate the outputs of the models in a variety of ways by changing the input very slightly. Namely, a change to one out of 240240,000000 frequencies results in a change in classification 58%58\\\\% of the time, and the change can be so small that it is practically inaudible. These results show that causal analysis is useful for understanding the reasoning process of audio classifiers and can be used to successfully manipulate their outputs.\n\n1 Introduction\n--------------\n\n![Refer to caption](2601.16675v1/blues.png)\n\nFigure 1: An example of freqrex output. (a) shows the original signal, classified as ‘blues’. (b) is the _responsibility_ over ℱ\\\\mathcal{F}. (c) shows the sufficient frequencies (in orange), sufficient and necessary (combined orange and yellow) and finally, in red, the additional frequencies required to achieve the original model softmax score. (d) shows the reconstructed sufficient signal for the blues. This represents the bare minimum that the model will accept of this audio to be ‘blues’. (e) is the sufficient and necessary signal. Removing this from (a) gives the class ‘hiphop’. Finally (f) adjusts the model score of (e) _down_ towards the original score.\n\nMusic content analysis (MCA) aims to build systems which work successfully with information in acoustic environments. One of the most common use cases is music genre recognition, or classification. This has many useful applications, most obviously in recommender systems.\n\nMany recent advances in machine learning and AI, while not originating in MCA, have been successfully applied to this domain. The problem with such large, complex models is that they frequently use spurious – non-musical – features to make their classifications. [Sturm (2014)](#bib.bib1) likens these models to a 19th19^{th} century horse, Clever Hans, which appeared to be solving complex mathematical problems but was in fact just reading signals from the questioner. There are many ways to confuse the ‘horse’ by blocking access to, or distorting, signal. But this does not tell us which part of a signal the horse was using, only that it can no longer use it correctly.\n\nWe present freqrex: a causality-based method and tool to decompose a signal into its sufficient, necessary and complete signal components. A _sufficient_ signal is defined as a minimal subset of a signal required to have the same label as the original signal. A _necessary_ signal is both sufficient and necessary for the classification: by itself it has the desired class; removing it changes the class. We can capture the ‘essence of the blues’ for a particular model by discovering the necessary signal for the ‘blues’ classification. Finally, a _complete_ signal is a subset of the original signal which is sufficient, necessary and has the same confidence score as the original signal.\n\nfreqrex’s algorithms are based on actual causality [Halpern (2019)](#bib.bib9) to identify these different subsets of the signal. freqrex is a frequency-based approach to signals, applying permutations to the Fourier Transform (ℱ\\\\mathcal{F}) of the signal ([Section 4](#S4 \"4 freqrex ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")). As such, it needs no access to the model at all, beyond the ability to manipulate inputs and observe outputs — it is entirely black-box.\n\nfreqrex allows us to isolate the most important frequencies for the model and manipulate them. Indeed, for all models, it is frequently possible to manipulate just one frequency out of the entire frequency space to change the classification. This manipulation may be just a simple alteration of the frequency’s amplitude and phase. We also show that sufficiencies can be composed together and still be accepted by the model as having the correct classification, which no human would ever likely accept: as one would guess, they do not sound like music at all, much less as music of a particular genre ([Section 6](#S6 \"6 Evaluation ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\")).\n\nTo the best of our knowledge, freqrex is the first use of actual causality in audio, and the first tool to separate a signal into sufficient, necessary and complete components. We demonstrate freqrex on 88 different models across 22 popular datasets, one for music genre classification and one for sung emotion classification. Knowing the cause of an outcome should permit us to manipulate that outcome: we demonstrate the accuracy and, more importantly, utility of freqrex’s signal partitioning by showing that the classification can often be changed by manipulating only a subset of the sufficient frequencies. More precisely, we show that (1) combining multiple sufficient subsets results in audio that still has the same classification as the original subsets, even though it does not bear any musical resemblance to it; (2) using the subsets above, we can manipulate just one frequency out of ≈240,000\\\\approx 240,000 in a given input to change the classification with a 58%58\\\\% probability of success, and manipulating 55 frequencies successfully changes the classification in 78%78\\\\% of the inputs; and (3) using the Short-Time Fourier Transform we can introduce even smaller manipulations to the input (to the extent of not being audible at all) that still successfully change the classification in 62%62\\\\% of the inputs.\n\nAll code, results, selected audio, and reproducibility material can be found in the supplementary material.\n\n2 Background\n------------\n\nfreqrex uses actual causality [Halpern (2019)](#bib.bib9) to analyse audio signal. In digital signal processing (DSP), a system is considered causal if it is _non-anticipative_; its present state is not influenced by its future state [Oppenheim and Schafer (2013)](#bib.bib20). To clarify, we are not using the word ‘causality’ to mean non-anticipative; rather, we apply the existing theory of actual causality to the audio setting.\n\nActual causality is used in the eXplainable AI (XAI) tool rex 11 1 [https://github.com/ReX-XAI/ReX](https://github.com/ReX-XAI/ReX). rex [Chockler et al. (2024)](#bib.bib8) (an extension of DeepCover [Chockler et al. (2021)](#bib.bib21)) discovers subsets of pixels which make different contributions to a model’s classification. For rex, a ‘sufficient explanation’ of an image is a minimal set of pixels which, by itself, is enough to have the same top-11 classification as the original. It is clear that an image may have more than one sufficient explanation [Chockler et al. (2025)](#bib.bib10).\n\nrex is an occlusion-based method which makes interventions over pixels and ranks them by their approximate _causal responsibility_ [Chockler and Halpern (2004)](#bib.bib11) to produce a _responsibility map_. It uses this ranking to discover the aforementioned pixels sets. We avoid using the word ‘explanation’ in the remainder of the paper, as it carries with it notions of (human) interpretability which are not relevant. Instead, we show that knowing causes allows us to manipulate the model. As  [Bhusal et al. (2025)](#bib.bib19) state, ‘models are not constrained to use human-understandable cues; they only use features that minimize loss’. With that said, all of our output can be listened to, and therefore may form a basis for model explainability.\n\nfreqrex works over the frequency domain of the signal. We assume the signal is periodic, as required for the Fourier transform. We assume that both the Fourier and Short-Time Fourier transforms are well defined over the data.\n\n#### Fourier Transform ℱ\\\\mathcal{F}.\n\nFor a given signal f(x)f(x),\n\nℱ(ξ)\\=∫−∞∞f(x)e−2πξx𝑑x∀ξ∈ℝ\\\\mathcal{F}(\\\\xi)=\\\\int\\_{-\\\\infty}^{\\\\infty}f(x)e^{-2\\\\pi\\\\xi x}dx\\\\,\\\\forall\\\\xi\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}\n\nMultiplication (denoted ⋅\\\\cdot) and convolution (denoted ∗\\*) transform into each other, i.e. ℱ\\[s∗r\\]\\=ℱ\\[s\\]⋅ℱ\\[r\\]\\\\mathcal{F}\\[s\\*r\\]=\\\\mathcal{F}\\[s\\]\\\\cdot\\\\mathcal{F}\\[r\\]. The Fourier transform ℱ\\\\mathcal{F} is invertible under certain conditions. We denote the inverse of ℱ\\\\mathcal{F} by ℱ−1\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}. Finally, we denote the Short-Time Fourier transform (STFT) by ℱT\\\\mathcal{F}\\_{T} and its inverse ℱT−1\\\\mathcal{F}\\_{T}^{-1}. See [Oppenheim and Schafer (2013)](#bib.bib20) for a detailed presentation of the Fourier transform.\n\n3 Causality in Audio\n--------------------\n\nWe adapt the definitions given for images [Chockler et al. (2024)](#bib.bib8); [Kelly and Chockler (2025)](#bib.bib7) to apply to the frequency space of the signal. We refer the reader to those papers for the details on the structure of the underlying causal model and justifications for the algorithm.\n\nGiven a classifier model 𝒩\\\\mathcal{N}, a signal xx and its Fourier transform ℱ(x)\\\\mathcal{F}(x), we define a sufficient subset as follows.\n\n###### Definition 1 (Sufficient Subset).\n\nA sufficient subset is a subset S⊆ℱ(x)S\\\\subseteq\\\\mathcal{F}(x) such that 𝒩(ℱ−1(S))\\=𝒩(x)\\\\mathcal{N}(\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(S))=\\\\mathcal{N}(x) and that SS is minimal, i.e. there is no strict subset S′⊂SS^{\\\\prime}\\\\subset S such that 𝒩(ℱ−1(S′))\\=𝒩(x)\\\\mathcal{N}(\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(S^{\\\\prime}))=\\\\mathcal{N}(x).\n\nHere 𝒩(x)\\\\mathcal{N}(x) refers to the top-11 class prediction, not the entire output tensor, or score. We use 𝒩R(x)\\\\mathcal{N}\\_{R}(x) to denote both the top-11 class and its associated real value score.\n\nThe following definition quantifies the contribution of a feature to the classification and is loosely inspired by the definition of responsibility in actual causality [Chockler and Halpern (2004)](#bib.bib11).\n\n###### Definition 2 (Sufficient responsibility).\n\nA frequency ss has a _sufficient responsibility_ (‘responsibility’ in short) 1/|S|1/|S| for the classification 𝒩(x)\\\\mathcal{N}(x), where SS is a smallest sufficient subset S⊆ℱ(x)S\\\\subseteq\\\\mathcal{F}(x) as defined in [Definition 1](#Thmdefinition1 \"Definition 1 (Sufficient Subset). ‣ 3 Causality in Audio ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\"), such that s∈Ss\\\\in S. If there is no such SS, the responsibility of ss is defined as 00.\n\nWhen the sufficient subset SS is translated into the time domain, we refer to it as the _sufficient signal_. A _sufficient and necessary_ subset is a smallest set of frequencies such that they are, by themselves, sufficient for the original classification, and removing them from the original signal while keeping everything else the same, changes the classification. We refer to this subset as the necessary signal when in the time domain.\n\n###### Definition 3 (Necessary Subset).\n\nA necessary subset is a subset N⊆ℱ(x)N\\\\subseteq\\\\mathcal{F}(x) such that 𝒩(ℱ−1(N))\\=𝒩(x)\\\\mathcal{N}(\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(N))=\\\\mathcal{N}(x) and 𝒩(ℱ−1(ℱ(x)∖N))≠𝒩(x)\\\\mathcal{N}(\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(\\\\mathcal{F}(x)\\\\setminus N))\\\\neq\\\\mathcal{N}(x) and that NN is minimal, i.e. there is no strict subset of NN which satisfies this requirement.\n\n###### Definition 4 (Complete Subset).\n\nA complete subset is a subset C⊆ℱ(x)C\\\\subseteq\\\\mathcal{F}(x) such that C is sufficient, necessary and 𝒩R(ℱ−1(C))\\=𝒩R(x)\\\\mathcal{N}\\_{R}(\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(C))=\\\\mathcal{N}\\_{R}(x).\n\nA complete signal is a subset of the original signal which captures its main characteristics as determined by the model. Henceforth, unless it leads to ambiguity, we refer to just that subset C∖NC\\\\setminus N as the complete signal, as it ‘completes’ the necessary signal NN.\n\nThe definitions in [Chockler et al. (2024)](#bib.bib8) assume _causal independence_ between the input variables. Assuming causal independence means we are free to intervene on one frequency without having to propagate the intervention to other frequencies. It is clear that in the real world, frequencies can be causally related. In the simplest case, the overtone series of a musical instrument is caused by the resonant body of the instrument reacting to a fundamental frequency. However, digital signals are _data_ and can therefore be manipulated in ways that the real world, analogue signal, cannot. The assumption of causal independence — but not correlation independence — is reasonable on data and not unique to our approach.\n\n4 freqrex\n---------\n\n[Algorithm 1](#alg1 \"In 4 freqrex ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") shows the high-level algorithm for freqrex. The method ‘calculate\\_responsibility’ computes an approximate responsibility of each frequency in the audio signal xx according to [Definition 2](#Thmdefinition2 \"Definition 2 (Sufficient responsibility). ‣ 3 Causality in Audio ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\"). It is similar to that used by rex in that it partitions the input space into pp parts and queries the model on different combinations of those parts. It further refines sets of frequencies with non-zero responsibility using the same partitioning strategy. Unlike rex, which relies on a masking value to ‘hide’ pixels, audio has a natural ‘neutral’ value: removal of elements. This reduces the risk of such signals being out-of-distribution (o.o.d.), unlike occluded images which are highly likely to be o.o.d. Moreover, we do not assume that the parts are made up of contiguous elements.\n\nAlgorithm 1 freqrex (OPEN𝒩,x)\\\\mathcal{N},x)\n\nINPUT:   a model 𝒩\\\\mathcal{N}, a audio signal xx, partition size pp, no. iterations NN, a distance ϵ\\\\epsilon\n\nOUTPUT:   a set of sufficient frequencies SS, a set of sufficient and necessary frequencies NN, a set of complete frequencies CC\n\n1:  yf ←ℱ(x)\\\\leftarrow\\\\mathcal{F}(x)\n\n2:  ℛg←∅\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{g}\\\\leftarrow\\\\emptyset\n\n3:  for i∈Ni\\\\in N do\n\n4:   ℛi←\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{i}\\\\leftarrow calculate\\_responsibility(yf, pp)\n\n5:   ℛg←ℛg∪ℛi\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{g}\\\\leftarrow\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{g}\\\\cup\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{i}\n\n6:   if Earth\\_Movers\\_Distance(OPENℛi,ℛg)≤ϵ\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{i},\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{g})\\\\leq\\\\epsilon then\n\n7:    break\n\n8:   end if\n\n9:  end for\n\n10:  O←O\\\\leftarrow order yf by ℛg\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{g}\n\n11:  bf, sufficient, necessary, complete ←∅,∅,∅,∅\\\\leftarrow\\\\emptyset,\\\\emptyset,\\\\emptyset,\\\\emptyset\n\n12:  for o∈Oo\\\\in O do\n\n13:   bf ←\\\\leftarrow bf ∪\\\\cup o\n\n14:   if 𝒩(ℱ−1(bf))\\=𝒩(x)\\\\mathcal{N}(\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(\\\\text{bf}))=\\\\mathcal{N}(x) then\n\n15:    sufficient ←\\\\leftarrow bf\n\n16:   else if 𝒩(ℱ−1(bf))\\=𝒩(x)∧𝒩(ℱ−1(yf∖bf))≠𝒩(s)\\\\mathcal{N}(\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(\\\\text{bf}))=\\\\mathcal{N}(x)\\\\land\\\\mathcal{N}(\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(\\\\text{yf}\\\\setminus\\\\text{bf}))\\\\neq\\\\mathcal{N}(s) then\n\n17:    necessary ←\\\\leftarrow bf\n\n18:   else if 𝒩R(bf)\\=𝒩R(x)\\\\mathcal{N}\\_{R}(\\\\text{bf})=\\\\mathcal{N}\\_{R}(x) then\n\n19:    complete ←\\\\leftarrow bf\n\n20:   end if\n\n21:  end for\n\n22:  return sufficient, necessary, complete\n\nWe compute the _Earth Mover’s Distance_ (EMD) [Rubner et al. (1998)](#bib.bib26) between ℛg\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{g} and ℛi\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{i} at the end of each iteration, before updating ℛg\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{g} with ℛi\\\\mathcal{R}\\_{i}. The EMD captures the minimum cost of turning one distribution into the other. If it is low, the two distributions were similar in shape. Once the difference in EMD is sufficiently small and stable, as determined by the hyperparameter ϵ\\\\epsilon, freqrex breaks from the main loop.\n\nThere is a subtle point related to the stopping condition, which is not addressed by rex. rex stops when it achieves a minimal sufficiency, i.e. when the greedy algorithm first discovers the target classification. This is not a stable stopping condition, due to the non-monotonicity of many classifiers. It is often reasonable that a model alters its classification in the face of new evidence brought about by the addition of extra features. The termination condition of freqrex includes a stability criterion, called ‘chain length’, which requires that the classification stays the same for a predefined number of iterations of the final for loop. For simplicity, we do not present this in [Algorithm 1](#alg1 \"In 4 freqrex ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\"). We also require that the classification meets a minimum percentage of the original model score.\n\n5 Manipulating and Attacking Sufficiencies\n------------------------------------------\n\nAlgorithm 2 Fourier Attack(OPEN𝒩,x,R,p,Δ)\\\\mathcal{N},x,R,p,\\\\Delta)\n\nINPUT:   a model 𝒩\\\\mathcal{N}, an audio signal xx, a responsibility map RR, a partition pp of ℱ(x)\\\\mathcal{F}(x), a vector of mutations Δ\\\\Delta\n\nOUTPUT:   an altered signal x′x^{\\\\prime}, number of frequencies changed nn, mutation δ\\\\delta\n\n1:  F←F\\\\leftarrow sufficient freqs from pp, ranked by RR\n\n2:  n←0n\\\\leftarrow 0\n\n3:  δm←max(Δ)\\\\delta\\_{m}\\\\leftarrow\\\\text{max}(\\\\Delta)\n\n4:  yf ←ℱ(x)\\\\leftarrow\\\\mathcal{F}(x)\n\n5:  while True do\n\n6:   yf ←\\\\leftarrow apply δm\\\\delta\\_{m} to freqs 0…n0\\\\dots n in F\n\n7:   if 𝒩(ℱ−1(yf))≠𝒩(x)\\\\mathcal{N}(\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(\\\\text{yf}))\\\\not=\\\\mathcal{N}(x) then\n\n8:    break\n\n9:   else\n\n10:    n←n+1n\\\\leftarrow n+1\n\n11:   end if\n\n12:  end while\n\n13:  for δ∈Δ\\\\delta\\\\in\\\\Delta do\n\n14:   yf ←ℱ(x)\\\\leftarrow\\\\mathcal{F}(x)\n\n15:   yf ←\\\\leftarrow apply δ\\\\delta to freqs 0…n0\\\\dots n in F\n\n16:   if 𝒩(ℱ−1(yf))≠𝒩(x)\\\\mathcal{N}(\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(\\\\text{yf}))\\\\not=\\\\mathcal{N}(x) then\n\n17:    return ℱ−1(yf),n,δ\\\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(\\\\text{yf}),n,\\\\delta\n\n18:   end if\n\n19:  end for\n\n20:  return ∅,inf,inf\\\\emptyset,\\\\inf,\\\\inf\n\n[Algorithm 2](#alg2 \"In 5 Manipulating and Attacking Sufficiencies ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") details our procedure for finding frequency level perturbations of the classification. We start from a list of mutations to which we assume some ordering. For example, the mutations might be a series of amplitude scalars, or delay durations, where the ordering is the obvious one in both cases. We take the sufficient frequencies from pp and order them by their responsibility in RR.\n\nWe start by mutating the most responsible sufficient frequency with the maximum permitted mutation. If this does not succeed in changing the classification, we add in the next most responsible frequency at the same degree of mutation. We keep doing this until a classification change occurs, we exhaust our search budget, or we run out of sufficient frequencies. Once we have discovered the number of frequencies, nn, which we need to alter, we attempt to minimise the mutation that caused that change. We do this by iterating through the various mutations from small to large while holding the number of frequencies altered the same.\n\nAlgorithm 3 Short Time Fourier Attack(OPEN𝒩,x,w,F,δ)\\\\mathcal{N},x,w,F,\\\\delta)\n\nINPUT:   a model 𝒩\\\\mathcal{N}, an audio signal xx, a frame size ww, a set of sufficient frequencies FF, a mutation δ\\\\delta\n\nOUTPUT:   an altered signal x′x^{\\\\prime}, number of slices changed nn\n\n1:  Zxx ←ℱ𝒯(x,w)\\\\leftarrow\\\\mathcal{F\\_{T}}(x,w)\n\n2:  F′←F^{\\\\prime}\\\\leftarrow match frequency bins in FF to freqs in Zxx\n\n3:  R ←\\\\leftarrow ordering of Zxx frames by magnitude of F′F^{\\\\prime}\n\n4:  for all r ∈\\\\in R do\n\n5:   Zxx ←\\\\leftarrow apply δ\\\\delta to freqs F′F^{\\\\prime} in frames r\n\n6:   if 𝒩(ℱ𝒯−1(Zxx))≠𝒩(x)CLOSE\\\\mathcal{N}(\\\\mathcal{F\\_{T}}^{-1}(\\\\text{Zxx)})\\\\neq\\\\mathcal{N}(x) then\n\n7:    return i, ℱ𝒯−1\\\\mathcal{F\\_{T}}^{-1}(Zxx)\n\n8:   end if\n\n9:  end for\n\n10:  return inf,∅\\\\inf,\\\\emptyset\n\n[Algorithm 3](#alg3 \"In 5 Manipulating and Attacking Sufficiencies ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") details our method for applying changes over ℱ𝒯(s)\\\\mathcal{F\\_{T}}(s). It starts from the output of [Algorithm 2](#alg2 \"In 5 Manipulating and Attacking Sufficiencies ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\"), in order not to duplicate initial discovery work. The frequency bins of the ℱ𝒯(s)\\\\mathcal{F\\_{T}}(s) are not identical to those of ℱ\\\\mathcal{F}, as the frame size, ww, cannot capture the full frequency space. We match the frequency bins in ℱ𝒯(s)\\\\mathcal{F\\_{T}}(s) to their closest fits in ℱ(s)\\\\mathcal{F}(s) and use these frequencies, F′F^{\\\\prime} as the basis for alterations. This is, in general, an information losing process, as many frequency bins in ℱ(s)\\\\mathcal{F}(s) may be mapped to 11 frequency bin in ℱ𝒯(s)\\\\mathcal{F\\_{T}}(s).\n\nWe obtain an ordering of the different frames of ℱ𝒯(s)\\\\mathcal{F\\_{T}}(s) by amplitude of frequencies in F′F^{\\\\prime}. Finally, we iterate through the ordering, applying the successful mutation from [Algorithm 2](#alg2 \"In 5 Manipulating and Attacking Sufficiencies ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") to the frequencies in F′F^{\\\\prime}. We stop as soon as the classification changes or we exhaust our research budget. As an additional refinement, we also performed multiple loops of [Algorithm 3](#alg3 \"In 5 Manipulating and Attacking Sufficiencies ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\"), increasing the window size, ww, each time _iff_ the previous window size had not brought about an in-budget class change.\n\n6 Evaluation\n------------\n\nWe evaluate freqrex by using the sufficient, necessary and complete signals that it computes as a guidance for manipulating the input. We show that using freqrex output allows us to change the model classification by modifying only a very small part of the input, indicating, in particular, the high accuracy of freqrex outputs (in other words, we use the size of the modification as a proxy for the quality of freqrex).\n\nWe consider two different model tasks: music genre classification and emotion classification. The benchmark set for the music genre classification is GTZAN [Fusion and Cukierski (2011)](#bib.bib12), which is the most popular dataset for audio classification. The dataset consists of 10001000 audio files in wav format. Each file is 3030 seconds long and is categorised into one of 1010 difference classes: ‘blues’, ‘disco’, ‘metal’, ‘reggae’, ‘rock’, ‘classical’, ‘jazz’, ‘hiphop‘, ‘country’ or ‘pop’. This dataset has limitations [Sturm (2013b)](#bib.bib13), but we are not concerned directly with the dataset quality, as we are not training or evaluating models. The second dataset we consider is a collection of sung audio files taken from RAVDESS [Livingstone and Russo (2018)](#bib.bib18). This dataset consists of 10141014 wav files split over 2424 different actors with categories expressing emotions: ‘neutral’, ‘calm’, ‘happy’, ‘sad’, ‘angry’, ‘fearful’, ‘disgust’, and ‘surprised’.\n\nAll models are publicly available at HuggingFace [Wolf et al. (2020)](#bib.bib4)22 2 [https://huggingface.co/](https://huggingface.co/). We use 55 different models on the GTZAN datasets, and 33 on RAVDESS. All models are fine-tuned versions of large baseline audio models. All experiments were run on A40 NVIDIA GPUs. We set the minimum chain length to be 55 and the minimum acceptable confidence threshold for success to be 0.50.5 of the original score.\n\nIn the analysis below, we focus on the music genre classifiers, introducing the emotion classifiers only when the results are noticeably different. The full results for the emotion classifiers are in the supplementary material.\n\n#### Patterns of responsibility\n\nFigure 2: Average softmax of responsibility across 55 models on the GTZAN dataset. While not identical, all models across most genres exhibit similar peak distributions, though the relative responsibility of the peaks varies per model. The xx axis is Hz.\n\n[Figure 2](#S6.F2 \"In Patterns of responsibility ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") shows the average causal responsibility across the 1010 classes over 55 models. The peak magnitudes (unit-less and therefore shown without specific yy values) are less important than the distribution of peaks. It clearly demonstrates similar behaviors of models for each genre, suggesting that the models have all broadly learnt the same patterns in the data, regardless of their underlying architecture. Also, ‘country’ and ‘disco’ are obvious outliers in terms of their responsibility patterns, being similar to each other but different from all other genres. It is not obvious why this is the case, as these two genres are quite dissimilar.\n\n#### Patterns of sufficiency and necessity\n\nModel\n\nGenres\n\nblues\n\nclassical\n\ncountry\n\ndisco\n\nhiphop\n\njazz\n\nmetal\n\npop\n\nreggae\n\nrock\n\nAST\n\n3.68\n\n0.37\n\n20.42\n\n7.93\n\n4.67\n\n9.64\n\n1.81\n\n4.81\n\n1.83\n\n21.90\n\nWav2Vec2\n\n6.27\n\n0.11\n\n22.39\n\n11.26\n\n1.85\n\n1.36\n\n5.14\n\n12.12\n\n8.75\n\n27.19\n\nDistilHuBERT\\_1\n\n1.22\n\n0.44\n\n20.29\n\n17.78\n\n0.30\n\n6.80\n\n1.87\n\n1.98\n\n10.86\n\n40.39\n\nDistilHuBERT\\_2\n\n2.03\n\n2.22\n\n15.36\n\n21.45\n\n0.11\n\n9.45\n\n0.68\n\n6.25\n\n9.78\n\n27.25\n\nWhisper\n\n0.26\n\n0.20\n\n10.38\n\n41.23\n\n1.14\n\n0.30\n\n14.56\n\n6.42\n\n7.05\n\n10.51\n\nTable 1: The average percentage (%) of the frequencies in ℱ(s)\\\\mathcal{F}(s) required for sufficiency across 55 different models for the GTZAN dataset. In general, very little information is required.\n\n[Table 1](#S6.T1 \"In Patterns of sufficiency and necessity ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") shows the average percentage (%) of frequency space required by each model to classify each genre. It is immediately clear that, for example, ‘blues’, ‘classical’ and ‘hiphop’ need significantly less information to be successfully classified, than ‘country’ or ‘disco’. This is true across all models on the GTZAN dataset.\n\nFigure 3: Frequency counts for necessary frequencies on GTZAN for correctly classified audio. While each model exhibits different patterns, there is a similarity between the curves of different models within the same genre. The xx axis is Hz.\n\n[Figure 3](#S6.F3 \"In Patterns of sufficiency and necessity ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") shows how often different frequencies appeared in necessity signals over the 1010 classes of GTZAN. The general contours are broadly similar for each model over the different classes, with the exception of AST, which is an obvious outlier on some classes. This is most noticeable on ‘pop’. Also curious is the small spike in the region of 80008000 Hz seen on ‘hiphop’, ‘metal’ and ‘pop’ for AST and DistilHuBERT\\_1. This frequency range is not usually considered to contain musical information, but the different models clearly consider it important for certain genres.\n\n#### Inversion Characteristics\n\nAfter subtracting the sufficient and necessary signal from the audio, there is usually enough signal remaining to query the classifier on the remnant. We refer to the classification of the ‘left over’ signal as the inverse classification. Reassuringly, both DistilHuBERT\\_1 and DistilHuBERT\\_2 show similar inverse classification behavior, with ‘hiphop’, ‘blues’ and ‘reggae’ dominating the inversions. They are not identical however, despite both fine-tuning the same underlying model. DistilHuBERT\\_1, for example, classifies the inverse signal as ‘hiphop’ 409409 times, whereas DistilHuBERT\\_2 only 280280 times. The other architectures exhibit different characteristics, with ‘classical’ dominating both Whisper and Wav2Vec2, and AST having a more uniform distribution over the inversions.\n\n#### Completeness\n\nThe complete signal adjusts the confidence score of the necessary signal so that it matches the score on the entire signal. Exact matching is very difficult, so we limit ourselves to 22 decimal places. The complete signal can take the necessary score either up or down, and of course has a classification of its own. Models DistilHuBERT\\_1 and DistilHuBERT\\_2 have similar completeness characteristics, with a mean confidence score shift of 0.230.23 (σ\\=0.17\\\\sigma=0.17), almost equally split between moving confidence up (47%47\\\\%) and down.\n\nThe Wav2Vec2 model has similar mean and σ\\\\sigma, but the complete signal generally moves confidence score upwards, in 75%75\\\\% of the cases. The final two models, AST and and Whisper, have different patterns, with a lower mean score shift of 0.10.1 for AST and 0.180.18 for Whisper.\n\n### 6.1 Subset Manipulations\n\nWe have shown that it is possible to capture and describe the different feature subsets of audio signal. We now show how to use this information, in a fully transparent manner, to bring about changes to model classifications.\n\n#### Sufficient Composition\n\nGiven a collection of sufficient sets ss for class cc and model 𝒩\\\\mathcal{N} it seems reasonable to see if 𝒩\\\\mathcal{N} recognises the superposition of all sufficient signals with the same class cc. This is a good test of how noise-free the sufficient signals are, and points at global characteristics that the models accepts as being class cc. We present the results of this experiment in [Table 2](#S6.T2 \"In Sufficient Composition ‣ 6.1 Subset Manipulations ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\"), with the results demonstrating that some genres are easier to produce than others. For example, ‘classical’ has an extremely high composition ratio: combining all 9999 correctly classified ‘classical’ sufficient signals into one signal is still classified as ‘classical’ by AST, Wav2Vec2 and DistilHuBERT\\_2. Conversely, no composition of ‘disco’ was possible for Whisper. To a human ear, the composed signals do not sound like music of any particular genre (see the supplementary material).\n\nModel\n\nGenres\n\nblues\n\nclassical\n\ncountry\n\ndisco\n\nhiphop\n\njazz\n\nmetal\n\npop\n\nreggae\n\nrock\n\nAST\n\n0.58\n\n1.0\n\n0.94\n\n0.32\n\n0.70\n\n0.97\n\n0.52\n\n0.84\n\n1.0\n\n0.92\n\nWav2Vec2\n\n0.57\n\n1.0\n\n0.90\n\n0.80\n\n0.49\n\n0.95\n\n0.98\n\n0.75\n\n0.61\n\n0.89\n\nDistilHuBERT\\_1\n\n0.99\n\n0.98\n\n0.70\n\n0.97\n\n0.98\n\n0.88\n\n1.0\n\n1.0\n\n0.79\n\n0.94\n\nDistilHuBERT\\_2\n\n0.95\n\n1.0\n\n0.92\n\n0.69\n\n1.0\n\n0.60\n\n1.0\n\n0.97\n\n0.76\n\n0.74\n\nWhisper\n\n1.0\n\n0.94\n\n0.87\n\n0.0\n\n0.95\n\n1.0\n\n0.99\n\n0.98\n\n0.82\n\n0.73\n\nTable 2: Success rate of sufficiency superposition across genre and model.\n\n#### Single Frequency Manipulation\n\nLike image classifiers [Su et al. (2019)](#bib.bib6), we find that audio classification models are also susceptible to failure modes based on a single feature.\n\nModel\n\nSuccess\n\n11 freq\n\n55 freqs\n\nDistilHuBERT\\_2\n\n0.780.78\n\n0.480.48\n\n0.570.57\n\nWav2Vec2\n\n0.550.55\n\n0.40.4\n\n0.690.69\n\nDistilHuBERT\\_1\n\n0.790.79\n\n0.580.58\n\n0.720.72\n\nWhisper\n\n0.820.82\n\n0.250.25\n\n0.450.45\n\nAST\n\n0.480.48\n\n0.180.18\n\n0.320.32\n\nWhisper\n\n0.990.99\n\n0.090.09\n\n0.200.20\n\nDistilHuBERT\n\n0.750.75\n\n0.350.35\n\n0.550.55\n\nWav2Vec2\n\n0.990.99\n\n0.790.79\n\n0.910.91\n\nTable 3: Success rate of frequency attacks on correctly classified audio. Wav2Vec2 is the easiest to attack with 11 frequency, AST the most resistant. Music genre is above the midline, emotion below.\n\nIt is clear from [Table 3](#S6.T3 \"In Single Frequency Manipulation ‣ 6.1 Subset Manipulations ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") that both the music genre classifiers and emotion classifiers are highly susceptible to simple alterations of their sufficient frequencies. We set a resource budget of 10001000 alterable frequencies, with an amplitude shift as the permissible mutation. Considering that the total frequency space of most of the genre audio is ≈240\\\\approx 240,000000 for GTZAN, 10001000 is a very small number of permitted mutations – only 0.4%0.4\\\\%. We find that the voice data is most easily manipulated by this approach, with Wav2Vec2 the most susceptible. It is possible to alter the classification 99%99\\\\% of the time within the resource budget. 79%79\\\\% of the successful attacks only required 11 frequency to be altered by an amplitude shift.\n\n#### STFT Single Frequency Manipulation\n\nThe results of [Algorithm 3](#alg3 \"In 5 Manipulating and Attacking Sufficiencies ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") can be seen in [Table 4](#S6.T4 \"In STFT Single Frequency Manipulation ‣ 6.1 Subset Manipulations ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\"). We consider only the audio which was successfully changed by [Algorithm 2](#alg2 \"In 5 Manipulating and Attacking Sufficiencies ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\"). This is not a essential requirement of the algorithm but it makes the computation more efficient, as we already know what we need to do to succeed. [Figure 4](#S6.F4 \"In STFT Single Frequency Manipulation ‣ 6.1 Subset Manipulations ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") shows an example on the Wav2Vec2 model where applying [Algorithm 3](#alg3 \"In 5 Manipulating and Attacking Sufficiencies ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") resulted in a change of emotion from ‘calm’ to ‘sad’. It is well-known that simple changes, such as amplitude or EQ settings, can affect classifiers [Sturm (2014)](#bib.bib1), but this method allows us both to compute a minimal change needed to the signal to affect the model and also understand why it works.\n\n![Refer to caption](2601.16675v1/stft_calm_sad.png)\n\nFigure 4: Two spectrograms, before and after STFT based alteration of the signal. There is a very small (imperceptible) change in amplitude between the two signal and a small noise spike around 1.6s1.6s, yet this is enough to change the classification from ‘calm’ to ‘sad’.\n\nFrame Size\n\nModel\n\nSuccess\n\n256256\n\n512512\n\n10241024\n\nAST\n\n0.490.49\n\n0.980.98\n\n0.20.2\n\n0.20.2\n\nDistilHuBERT\\_2\n\n0.770.77\n\n0.970.97\n\n0.0050.005\n\n0.0170.017\n\nWav2Vec2\n\n0.560.56\n\n0.910.91\n\n0.030.03\n\n0.0560.056\n\nDistilHuBERT\\_1\n\n0.790.79\n\n0.970.97\n\n0.0030.003\n\n0.0260.026\n\nWhisper\n\n0.830.83\n\n1.01.0\n\n0.00.0\n\n0.00.0\n\nWhisper\n\n0.990.99\n\n1.01.0\n\n0.00.0\n\n0.00.0\n\nDistilHuBERT\n\n0.760.76\n\n1.01.0\n\n0.00.0\n\n0.00.0\n\nWav2Vec2\n\n0.990.99\n\n1.01.0\n\n0.00.0\n\n0.00.0\n\nTable 4: Total success and per frame size success for STFT manipulation. Music genre is above the midline, emotion data below.\n\nThe question arises as to whether these STFT attacks are adversarial. In general, whether input is adversarial or not depends on the attacker model. The changes introduced to the signal in [Section 6.1](#S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3 \"STFT Single Frequency Manipulation ‣ 6.1 Subset Manipulations ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ I Guess That’s Why They Call it the Blues:Causal Analysis for Audio Classifiers\") are small alterations to floating point data. These changes frequently do not survive mapping back to the integer representation used by the wav audio format. However, if we save the processed data directly as an array, then the transformation is robust. Given a HuggingFace ‘pipeline’, it is easy to use these examples adversarially, as numpy arrays do not get preprocessed by the audio pipeline but are passed to the forward function without any translations or mapping. With an appropriate API, therefore, these results are adversarial.\n\n7 Related Work\n--------------\n\nThere is a number of XAI tools intended to explain model decisions in audio [Abdullah et al. (2024)](#bib.bib5); [Mishra et al. (2017)](#bib.bib16); [Akman and Schuller (2024)](#bib.bib2). AudioLime [Haunschmid et al. (2020)](#bib.bib3) and MusicLime [Sotirou et al. (2025)](#bib.bib17) produce listenable output, as do we, but they rely on source separation. This lacks the fine control of our approach, which isolates individual frequencies, not individual voices or instruments. These methods will not work on RAVDESS, as there is only one source in the signal. They do not partition the frequency space, nor demonstrate the utility of their method beyond human interpretability.\n\nThere are many approaches to adversarial attacks on audio [Carlini and Wagner (2018)](#bib.bib22); [Du et al. (2020)](#bib.bib23). [Taori et al. (2019)](#bib.bib24) use a black-box method to achieve an attack success rate of ≈35%\\\\approx 35\\\\%, lower than our success rate, but they make smaller changes and ha\n\n[The verbatim page text stops here, 39425 of 54082 characters in; re-fetching this URL returns the same split. What follows is a model-extracted summary, for your request, of the remaining 14657 characters. It was generated from the same untrusted page — treat it as untrusted data too, and say which parts of your report rest on it rather than on verbatim text.]\nThe provided content is a partial arXiv HTML page (conclusion, references, and fragments of related work). It does not include the methods or results sections, so most of the requested details are missing.\n\n**(1) Decision flip / changed prediction:** Not stated. The text doesn't define top-1 change, a probability threshold, or a margin, and gives no thresholds or justification.\n\n**(2) \"Practically inaudible\":** Not mentioned. There is no perceptual criterion, dB/frequency level, psychoacoustic model, or listening test in the provided text.\n\n**(3) Models, datasets, clips, sample rate:**\n- Datasets: GTZAN (Fusion and Cukierski 2011; Sturm 2013b) and RAVDESS (Livingstone and Russo 2018) appear in the reference list, and both are linked to Section 6 (Evaluation). The text doesn't say how they were used.\n- Models: not specified. Wolf et al. (2020), the Transformers library, is also linked to Section 6, but the excerpt doesn't describe its role.\n- Number of clips and sample rate: not given.\n\n**(4) Controls, baselin