{"parentUuid":"68d92fd9-0472-48b6-88af-2e7450d933b0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a57da868928d8ed27","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkZgjVSUiZnfnngDvZUW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I read the arXiv HTML version (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.16675) but only about 73% of it verbatim. The PDF (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.16675) did not yield readable text, so I did not use it. Rough coverage: the verbatim text runs through Sections 1 to 6.1, cut off partway through the Related Work section, and the remaining references and conclusion were summarized by a model. That summary said the methods and results were missing, which contradicts the verbatim text, so I have ignored it except where noted. Quotes are kept under 125 characters.\n\n**1. What counts as a decision flip**\n- The paper does not use the phrase \"decision flip.\" Its \"changed classification\" is a top-1 change. Definition 1 says \"𝒩(x) refers to the top-1 class prediction, not the entire output tensor, or score,\" and Definition 3 requires \"𝒩(ℱ⁻¹(ℱ(x)∖N)) ≠ 𝒩(x).\"\n- The attack loop (Algorithm 2) stops on \"𝒩(ℱ⁻¹(yf)) ≠ 𝒩(x)\", which is a plain top-1 inequality with no margin.\n- A score threshold applies separately. Definition 4 (\"complete subset\") requires the top-1 class and its score to match: \"𝒩_R(ℱ⁻¹(C)) = 𝒩_R(x).\"\n- Thresholds (Section 6): \"We set the minimum chain length to be 5 and the minimum acceptable confidence threshold for success to be 0.5 of the original score.\" No justification is given for 5 or 0.5. Section 4 says only that \"We also require that the classification meets a minimum percentage of the original model score.\"\n- Section 4 also says the stopping rule uses an EMD tolerance ε, but no value for ε is given in the text I read.\n\n**2. \"Practically inaudible\"**\n- The phrase appears only in the abstract: \"the change can be so small that it is practically inaudible.\"\n- The introduction says the STFT manipulations go \"to the extent of not being audible at all.\"\n- The only other support is the Figure 4 caption: \"There is a very small (imperceptible) change in amplitude between the two signal and a small noise spike around 1.6s.\"\n- I found no dB level, SNR, psychoacoustic model, loudness measure, or listening test in the verbatim text. Inaudibility is asserted, not measured.\n- Section 6.1 undercuts the claim: \"These changes frequently do not survive mapping back to the integer representation used by the wav audio format.\" The authors say the results are adversarial \"if we save the processed data directly as an array.\"\n- Section 6 also says: \"we are not concerned directly with the dataset quality\" and calls the composed sufficient signals ones that \"do not sound like music of any particular genre.\" Those are informal listening judgments, not a test.\n\n**3. Models, datasets, sample rate**\n- Music genre (Section 6): \"We use 5 different models on the GTZAN datasets, and 3 on RAVDESS.\" The genre models named in the tables are AST, Wav2Vec2, DistilHuBERT_1, DistilHuBERT_2, and Whisper.\n- The emotion models appear in the tables as Whisper, DistilHuBERT, and Wav2Vec2 (the table labels are ambiguous).\n- The paper says the models are \"fine-tuned versions of large baseline audio models,\" hosted on HuggingFace.\n- GTZAN: \"The dataset consists of 1000 audio files in wav format. Each file is 30 seconds long,\" in 10 genres.\n- RAVDESS: \"This dataset consists of 1014 wav files split over 24 different actors,\" with 8 emotions. The paper calls these \"sung audio files.\"\n- Sample rate: not stated in the verbatim text.\n- Number of clips actually attacked: not given as a total. Table 2 refers to per-genre counts (for example, \"all 99 correctly classified 'classical' sufficient signals\").\n- Internal inconsistency: the introduction says \"We demonstrate freqrex on 88 different models across 22 popular datasets.\" Section 6 gives 5 + 3 models on 2 datasets. The 88/22 figure looks like a rendering error or a mistake, and I could not resolve it.\n\n**4. Controls, baselines, statistics**\n- I found no random-perturbation baseline, no random-frequency control, and no comparison to a non-causal ranking in the verbatim text.\n- The only external comparison is to Taori et al. (2019): \"[Taori et al. (2019)] use a black-box method to achieve an attack success rate of ≈35%, lower than our success rate, but they make smaller changes\" (sentence truncated in the source).\n- No confidence intervals, standard errors, or significance tests appear. The only spread reported is for completeness: \"a mean confidence score shift of 0.23 (σ=0.17).\"\n- Figure 2 and the Table 1 and Table 2 values are reported as averages or percentages with no uncertainty.\n\n**5. Headline numbers (abstract and introduction)**\n- Abstract: \"a change to one out of 240,000 frequencies results in a change in classification 58% of the time.\"\n- Introduction, item (2): one frequency (about 240,000 available) gives \"a 58% probability of success,\" and \"manipulating 5 frequencies successfully changes the classification in 78% of the inputs.\"\n- Introduction, item (3): with the STFT, \"successfully change the classification in 62% of the inputs.\"\n- Section 6.1: a budget of \"1000 alterable frequencies\" is \"only 0.4%\" of the total. \"It is possible to alter the classification 99% of the time within the resource budget\" (Wav2Vec2, emotion). \"79% of the successful attacks only required 1 frequency.\"\n- Caveat: Table 3 and Table 4 have column headers and row labels that the text extraction garbled (Whisper and Wav2Vec2 each appear twice). I could not reliably tie the 58%, 78%, and 62% headline figures to specific table cells, so treat them as the paper's stated numbers.\n- The paper gives no sample rate or total number of attacked clips, so the 58% and 62% rates cannot be checked against a denominator.\n\n**6. Stated limitations (verbatim)**\n- Causal independence: \"The definitions in [Chockler et al. (2024)] assume causal independence between the input variables.\" The authors add that \"frequencies can be causally related\" in the real world.\n- Stopping rule: the classic rex stopping condition \"is not a stable stopping condition, due to the non-monotonicity of many classifiers.\"\n- STFT approximation: mapping STFT bins to FFT bins \"is, in general, an information losing process.\"\n- Quantization: the STFT changes \"frequently do not survive mapping back to the integer representation.\"\n- Adversarial status: \"whether input is adversarial or not depends on the attacker model.\"\n- Assumed periodic signals: \"We assume the signal is periodic, as required for the Fourier transform.\"\n- Dataset: \"This dataset has limitations [Sturm (2013b)], but we are not concerned directly with the dataset quality.\"\n\n**Source notes**\n- The model-written summary of the unread tail (which I did not rely on) gave a sentence attributed to the paper's conclusion: \"Our main goal is discoverability and utility of causes in audio, not adversarial attacks.\" I could not confirm it verbatim, so treat it as unverified.\n- The summary's \"11 frequency\" phrase also looks like a rendering error and is unverified.\n- The PDF was saved locally by the fetch tool, but I could not read it. Sample rate, the EMD ε value, the chain-length details, the exact attack-budget procedure, and the remaining results appear only in the unread portion or the PDF. Reading the full PDF text (for example via pdftotext) would be the next step to fill those gaps."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":47421,"cache_read_input_tokens":3804,"output_tokens":6383,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3774},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m