lerance ε, but no value for ε appears in the text I read.\n\n2. \"Practically inaudible\"\n- The phrase appears only in the abstract: \"the change can be so small that it is practically inaudible.\"\n- The introduction says the STFT manipulations go \"to the extent of not being audible at all.\"\n- The only other support is the Figure 4 caption: \"There is a very small (imperceptible) change in amplitude between the two signal and a small noise spike around 1.6s.\"\n- I found no dB level, SNR, psychoacoustic model, loudness measure, or listening test in the verbatim text. Inaudibility is asserted, not measured.\n- Section 6.1 undercuts the claim: \"These changes frequently do not survive mapping back to the integer representation used by the wav audio format.\" The authors say the attacks are adversarial \"if we save the processed data directly as an array.\"\n- Informal listening judgments appear in Section 6, for example that composed sufficient signals \"do not sound like music of any particular genre.\" These are not a test.\n\n3. Models, datasets, sample rate\n- Music genre (Section 6): \"We use 5 different models on the GTZAN datasets, and 3 on RAVDESS.\" Genre models named in the tables: AST, Wav2Vec2, DistilHuBERT_1, DistilHuBERT_2, Whisper.\n- Emotion models appear in the tables as Whisper, DistilHuBERT, and Wav2Vec2 (table labels are ambiguous).\n- The models are \"fine-tuned versions of large baseline audio models,\" hosted on HuggingFace.\n- GTZAN: \"The dataset consists of 1000 audio files in wav format. Each file is 30 seconds long,\" in 10 genres.\n- RAVDESS: \"This dataset consists of 1014 wav files split over 24 different actors,\" with 8 emotions, described as sung audio.\n- Sample rate: not stated in the verbatim text.\n- Number of clips actually attacked: no total is given. Table 2 refers to per-genre counts (for example, \"all 99 correctly classified 'classical' sufficient signals\").\n- Inconsistency: the introduction says \"We demonstrate freqrex on 88 different models across 22 popular datasets.\" Section 6 gives 5 + 3 models on 2 datasets. The 88/22 figure looks like a rendering error or a mistake, and I could not resolve it.\n\n4. Controls, baselines, statistics\n- I found no random-perturbation baseline, no random-frequency control, and no comparison to a non-causal ranking in the verbatim text.\n- The only external comparison is to Taori et al. (2019): \"[Taori et al. (2019)] use a black-box method to achieve an attack success rate of ≈35%, lower than our success rate, but they make smaller changes\" (the sentence is truncated in the source).\n- No confidence intervals, standard errors, or significance tests appear. The only spread reported is for completeness: \"a mean confidence score shift of 0.23 (σ=0.17).\"\n- Tables 1 and 2 and Figure 2 report averages or percentages with no uncertainty.\n\n5. Headline numbers\n- Abstract: \"a change to one out of 240,000 frequencies results in a change in classification 58% of the time.\"\n- Introduction, item (2): one frequency (out of about 240,000) gives \"a 58% probability of success,\" and \"manipulating 5 frequencies successfully changes the classification in 78% of the inputs.\"\n- Introduction, item (3): with the STFT, \"successfully change the classification in 62% of the inputs.\"\n- Section 6.1: a budget of \"1000 alterable frequencies\" is \"only 0.4%\" of the total. \"It is possible to alter the classification 99% of the time within the resource budget\" (Wav2Vec2, emotion). \"79% of the successful attacks only required 1 frequency to be altered.\"\n- Caveat: text extraction garbled Tables 3 and 4 (column headers unclear, Whisper and Wav2Vec2 each appear twice). I could not reliably map the 58%, 78%, and 62% figures to specific table cells, so treat them as the paper's stated numbers.\n- No sample rate or total attacked-clip count is given, so the 58% and 62% rates cannot be checked against a denominator.\n\n6. Stated limitations (verbatim)\n- Causal independence: \"The definitions in [Chockler et al. (2024)] assume causal independence between the input variables.\" The authors add that frequencies \"can be causally related\" in the real world.\n- Stopping rule: the rex stopping condition \"is not a stable stopping condition, due to the non-monotonicity of many classifiers.\"\n- STFT approximation: mapping STFT bins to FFT bins \"is, in general, an information losing process.\"\n- Quantization: the STFT changes \"frequently do not survive mapping back to the integer representation.\"\n- Adversarial status: \"whether input is adversarial or not depends on the attacker model.\"\n- Periodicity: \"We assume the signal is periodic, as required for the Fourier transform.\"\n- Dataset: \"This dataset has limitations [Sturm (2013b)], but we are not concerned directly with the dataset quality.\"\n\nSource notes\n- The model-written summary of the unread tail gave a sentence attributed to the conclusion: \"Our main goal is discoverability and utility of causes in audio, not adversarial attacks.\" I could not confirm it verbatim, so treat it as unverified. Its \"11 frequency\" phrase also looks like a rendering error and is unverified. I did not rely on either.\n- Not available from the text I read: sample rate, the EMD ε value, exact chain-length semantics, the full attack-budget procedure, and the remaining results. These are in the unread tail or the PDF. Extracting the PDF text (for example with pdftotext) is the next step to fill these gaps."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"9fce7e17-f48d-4804-bea6-e9ddd5766062","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgkZiq95PJJPce18rW6dQ","requestRef":"4eaac806-ae2a-489b-a058-72d7e3c209fd","requestedModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"5m","attributionAgent":"web-fetch","type":"assistant","uuid":"4eb8018a-0846-4ec4-aae4-f91cab0e0ac3","timestamp":"2026-11-05T22:24:40.902Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","e